window.docContents[232].resolve({"/Appendix/#section-chapter-13-theorems":{"contents":"* IsCompleteMultipartite.finpartition: the parts of a finite complete multipartite\ngraph, as a Finpartition of its vertices.* IsCopyExtremal: G has property p and the most copies of H among the graphs\non its vertices with property p, generalizing Mathlib's IsExtremal from edges.* IsNCliqueMaximal: G is s-clique-maximal, that is, (r + 1)-clique-free and\nwith the most s-cliques among such graphs on its vertices.* cliqueUnion: the union of the s-cliques of G; distinct vertices are adjacent\nwhen they lie in a common s-clique.* paleyGraph₉: the Paley graph on 9 vertices, as the circulant graph on\nZMod 3 × ZMod 3 in which two vertices are adjacent when they agree in one\ncoordinate.* IsDirection: a direction of G, a consistent choice of a component of G - K\nfor every finite set K of vertices (the component for L lies inside the one\nfor K whenever K ⊆ L).\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Appendix\u0009Definitions","header":"Chapter 13: Theorems","id":"/Appendix/#section-chapter-13-theorems"},"/Cliques/#section-clique-number-of-the-mycielskian":{"contents":"We now turn to the clique number of the Mycielskian.\n\nOn paper this is easy: any clique in G also appears in mμ[G] via the obvious\nembedding. Conversely any clique of size at least 3 in mμ[G] cannot use the\nvertex none since none is only adjacent to the vertices in the right copy of V,\nand the right copy of V is an independent set. Moreover no clique in mμ[G] can\ncontain v ₗ and v ᵣ for any v ∈ V. (This would imply G.Adj v v, which is\nimpossible.) Hence any clique of size at least 3 in mμ[G] can be mapped to a\nclique of the same size in G using the projection we will define below.\n\nWe first prove that the Mycielskian embedding mapping G into its Mycielskian sends\nk-cliques in G to k-cliques in mμ[G].\n\nlemma IsNClique.mycielskian {k : ℕ} {s : Finset V} (h : G.IsNClique k s) :\n    (mμ[G]).IsNClique k (s.map (Embedding.mycielskianLeft G).toEmbedding) := by\n  classical\n  simpa [Finset.map_eq_image] using h.image (Embedding.mycielskianLeft G).toHom\n\n\nNext we want to show that the vertex none cannot belong to any k-clique in\nmμ[G] when 3 ≤ k, because none is only adjacent to right vertices, and no two\nright vertices are adjacent.\n\nlemma mycielskian_none_notMem_clique {k : ℕ} (h3 : 3 ≤ k)\n    {t : Finset (Option (V ⊕ V))} (ht : mμ[G].IsNClique k t) : none ∉ t := by\n  classical -- needed to use Finset.erase below since it requires DecidableEq V\n  intro hmem\n  -- since t is a k-clique with 3 ≤ k, t.erase none has at least 2 elements.\n  have hcard : 2 ≤ (t.erase none).card := by\n    grind [Finset.card_erase_of_mem hmem, ht.card_eq]\n  -- so we can find two distinct vertices a, b in t.erase none, and they are\n  -- adjacent since t is a clique.\n  obtain ⟨a, ha, b, hb, hab⟩ := Finset.one_lt_card.mp hcard\n  -- both a and b are adjacent to none in mμ[G]\n  have h_na := ht.isClique hmem (Finset.mem_of_mem_erase ha) (by grind)\n  have h_nb := ht.isClique hmem (Finset.mem_of_mem_erase hb) (by grind)\n  -- so they must be \"right\" vertices\n  -- below try tac runs tac, but if tac fails it\n  -- does nothing instead of raising an error\n  rcases a with _ | ⟨va | va⟩ <;> rcases b with _ | ⟨vb | vb⟩\n    <;> try simp only [mycielskian_adj] at h_na h_nb\n  -- but no two \"right\" vertices are adjacent, so we have a contradiction.\n  exact ht.isClique (Finset.mem_of_mem_erase ha) (Finset.mem_of_mem_erase hb) hab\n\n\nIf s is a clique in the Mycielskian then s cannot contain both v ₗ and v ᵣ\nfor any v ∈ V (since mμ[G].Adj v ₗ v ᵣ would imply G.Adj v v, which is\nimpossible). Moreover we just proved that any k-clique with 3 ≤ k cannot contain\nnone. This means that if we define a projection map from the vertices of mμ[G] to\nthe vertices of G by \"forgetting\" which side (left or right) vertices came from\n(and sending none to an arbitrary vertex in V) then it will be injective on any\nclique in mμ[G] of size at least 3 and also preserve adjacency.\n\nProjection from the vertex set of mμ[G] to that of G (preserves k-cliques\nfor k ≥ 3)./-- Projection from the vertex set of mμ[G] to that of G (preserves k-cliques\nfor k ≥ 3). -/\ndef mycielskianProj (v₀ : V) : Option (V ⊕ V) → V\n  | none => v₀\n  | v ₗ => v\n  | v ᵣ => v\n\n\nWe need some simple definitional lemmas about mycielskianProj.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma mycielskianProj_left (v₀ v : V) :\n    mycielskianProj v₀ v ₗ = v := rfl\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma mycielskianProj_right (v₀ v : V) :\n    mycielskianProj v₀ v ᵣ = v := rfl\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma mycielskianProj_none (v₀ : V) : mycielskianProj v₀ none = v₀ := rfl\n\n\nAny k-clique in mμ[G] (with 3 ≤ k) maps to a k-clique in G under\nmycielskianProj. Such a clique doesn't contain none, and every edge of mμ[G]\nnot involving none projects to an edge of G.\n\nlemma IsNClique.of_mycielskian [DecidableEq V] (v₀ : V) {k : ℕ} (h3 : 3 ≤ k)\n    {t : Finset (Option (V ⊕ V))} (ht : mμ[G].IsNClique k t) :\n    G.IsNClique k (t.image (mycielskianProj v₀)) := by\n  -- any clique of size at least 3 in mμ[G] cannot contain none\n  have hnone := mycielskian_none_notMem_clique h3 ht\n  -- so it suffices to show that the projection preserves adjacency on t\n  refine ht.image_of_adj fun x hx y hy hadj => ?_\n  -- first | t₁ | t₂ | … tries the tactics in turn, until one succeeds\n  rcases x with _ | ⟨_ | _⟩ <;> rcases y with _ | ⟨_ | _⟩ <;> -- 9 goals\n    first | grind | exact hadj | contradiction\n\n\nThis generalizes CliqueFree.mycielskian: for every r ≥ 3, the Mycielskian of an\nr-clique-free graph is r-clique-free. No finiteness is needed. To apply\nIsNClique.of_mycielskian we need a vertex v₀ : V, and the clique itself provides\none: it has a vertex, which is not none, so it is v ₗ or v ᵣ for some v ∈ V.\nThe bound 3 ≤ r cannot be dropped. The empty graph on one vertex is\n2-clique-free, but its Mycielskian has an edge from none to the right copy of the\nvertex.\n\ntheorem CliqueFree.mycielskian_of_three_le {r : ℕ} (hr : 3 ≤ r)\n    (h : G.CliqueFree r) : mμ[G].CliqueFree r := by\n  classical -- to infer DecidableEq V\n  intro t ht\n  -- an r-clique with 3 ≤ r has a vertex x\n  obtain ⟨x, hx⟩ : t.Nonempty := by grind [Finset.card_pos, ht.card_eq]\n  rcases x with _ | ⟨v | v⟩\n  · exact mycielskian_none_notMem_clique hr ht hx\n  -- if x is v ₗ or v ᵣ then t projects to an r-clique in G\n  all_goals exact h _ (ht.of_mycielskian v hr)\n\n\nAny finite graph with at least one edge has the same clique number as its\nMycielskian. (For the edgeless graph ⊥ the answer depends on whether V is empty;\nsee the exercises.)\n\ntheorem cliqueNum_mycielskian_of_ne_bot [Finite V] (h : G ≠ ⊥) :\n    ω mμ[G] = ω G := by\n  classical -- to infer DecidableEq V\n  -- G ≠ ⊥ gives an edge, i.e. a 2-clique, so 2 ≤ ω G\n  obtain ⟨a, b, hab⟩ := ne_bot_iff_exists_adj.mp h\n  have h2 : 2 ≤ ω G := (G.is2Clique_pair_iff.mpr hab).le_cliqueNum\n  -- G is (ω G + 1)-clique-free, so mμ[G] is too since 3 ≤ ω G + 1\n  have hfree : G.CliqueFree (ω G + 1) := fun _ hs => by grind [hs.le_cliqueNum]\n  -- and a maximum clique of G maps to an ω G-clique in mμ[G]\n  obtain ⟨s, hs⟩ := G.exists_isNClique_cliqueNum\n  exact hs.mycielskian.cliqueNum_eq_of_cliqueFree\n    (hfree.mycielskian_of_three_le (by grind))\n\n\nApplying this repeatedly to K[2], which has clique number 2 by cliqueNum_top,\nwe see that every iterated Mycielskian of K[2] has clique number 2. Each\nmμ^n K[2] has an edge, since K[2] does and the left copies of the ends of an edge\nof G are adjacent in mμ[G].\n\ntheorem cliqueNum_iterMycielskian_completeGraph_two (n : ℕ) :\n    ω (mμ^n K[2]) = 2 := by\n  have hne (n : ℕ) : mμ^n K[2] ≠ ⊥ := by\n    induction n with\n    | zero => exact (ne_bot_iff_exists_adj.mpr ⟨0, 1, by decide⟩ : K[2] ≠ ⊥)\n    | succ n ih =>\n      obtain ⟨a, b, hab⟩ := ne_bot_iff_exists_adj.mp ih\n      exact ne_bot_iff_exists_adj.mpr ⟨a ₗ, b ₗ, hab⟩\n  induction n with\n  | zero => simp\n  | succ n ih => rwa [iterMycielskian_succ, cliqueNum_mycielskian_of_ne_bot (hne n)]\n\nend mycielskian_cliquenum\n\n\nIn Colouring Mycielskians we show that mμ^n K[2] has chromatic\nnumber n + 2. So these graphs all have clique number 2 but have arbitrarily large\nchromatic number.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Cliques\u0009Clique and Independence Number of the Mycielskian","header":"6.7.2. Clique Number of the Mycielskian","id":"/Cliques/#section-clique-number-of-the-mycielskian"},"/Cliques/#section-independence-number-of-the-infinite-path-on":{"contents":"The infinite path graph on ℕ (pathNat₁) has unbounded independent sets, so what\nis its independence number? Due to the sSup definition, it is 0!\n\nAdjacent vertices of pathNat₁ differ by one, so they have different parities. Hence\nany set of natural numbers that all have the same parity is independent.\n\nlemma isIndepSet_pathNat₁_of_mod_two_eq {s : Set ℕ}\n    (h : ∀ ⦃a b⦄, a ∈ s → b ∈ s → a % 2 = b % 2) : pathNat₁.IsIndepSet s := by\n  rw [isIndepSet_iff_forall_not_adj]\n  rintro a b ha hb (rfl | rfl) <;> grind [h ha hb]\n\n\nIn particular, for every n the first n even numbers form an n-independent set.\nSo the set of sizes of independent sets is not bounded above, and\nNat.sSup_of_not_bddAbove gives ⍺ pathNat₁ = 0.\n\nlemma indepNum_pathNat₁ : ⍺ pathNat₁ = 0 := by\n  apply Nat.sSup_of_not_bddAbove\n  rintro ⟨N, hN⟩\n  have hs : pathNat₁.IsNIndepSet (N + 1)\n      ((Finset.range (N + 1)).map ⟨(2 * ·), mul_right_injective₀ two_ne_zero⟩) := by\n    refine ⟨isIndepSet_pathNat₁_of_mod_two_eq ?_, by simp⟩\n    simp only [Finset.coe_map, Function.Embedding.coeFn_mk, Set.mem_image]\n    grind\n  exact N.not_succ_le_self (hN ⟨_, hs⟩)\n\nend indset\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Cliques\u0009Independent Sets","header":"6.6.2. Independence Number of the Infinite Path on ℕ","id":"/Cliques/#section-independence-number-of-the-infinite-path-on"},"/Constructions/#section-complete-and-empty-graphs":{"contents":"Given a vertex set V the complete graph on V has all possible edges while the\nempty graph on V has no edges.\n\nIn Mathlib these are usually referred to by their roles as the top ⊤ and bottom ⊥\nelements of the complete atomic Boolean algebra formed by SimpleGraph V (see\nOrder for more details).\n\n#source completeGraph\n#source emptyGraph\n\n\nAs we saw in Top and Bottom, results about these graphs use the\ntop and bot names, as in top_adj and bot_adj.\n\nThe complete graphs K_3 and K_5\n\nRecall that Fin n is the type of natural numbers less than n with addition mod\nn. (In particular Fin 0 is the empty type.)\n\nWe can construct the complete graph on Fin n as follows.\n\nexample (n : ℕ) : SimpleGraph (Fin n) := completeGraph (Fin n)\n\n\nWe can introduce the notation K[n] for the complete graph on Fin n as follows.\n(The keyword scoped makes the notation available only inside\nnamespace SimpleGraph, or wherever SimpleGraph is opened.)\n\nThe complete graph on Fin n is denoted K[n]./-- The complete graph on Fin n is denoted K[n]. -/\nscoped notation \"K[\" n \"]\" => completeGraph (Fin n)\n\n\nSince Fin n has decidable equality, K[n] has decidable adjacency: if we have a\nprocedure for deciding whether two vertices are equal then we can decide whether\nthey are adjacent in the complete graph, since adjacency is simply not-equal-to ≠.\n\nFor example, 10 and 111 are adjacent in K[417].\n\nexample : K[417].Adj 10 111 := by decide\n\n\nWe can similarly introduce the notation E[n] for the empty graph on Fin n.\n\nThe empty graph on Fin n is denoted E[n]./-- The empty graph on Fin n is denoted E[n]. -/\nscoped notation \"E[\" n \"]\" => emptyGraph (Fin n)\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Constructions\u0009Basic Families of Graphs","header":"3.1.1. Complete and Empty Graphs","id":"/Constructions/#section-complete-and-empty-graphs"},"/Constructions/#section-hypercube":{"contents":"Often there is more than one way to construct a particular graph. A good example of\nthis is the n-dimensional hypercube, which can be constructed in many different\nways: as the Hasse diagram of the Boolean algebra of subsets of an n-element set;\nas the graph on Fin n → Bool with edges between vertices that differ in exactly one\ncoordinate (i.e. at Hamming distance one R. W. Hamming, Error detecting and error correcting codes, Bell System Tech. J. 29 (1950), no. 2, 147–160.); or as the box product\nof n copies of K₂.\n\nThe Hasse diagram construction is very easy to give, but then is a pain to work with.\n\nThe Hasse diagram construction of the n-dimensional hypercube./-- The Hasse diagram construction of the n-dimensional hypercube. -/\nabbrev hypercubeHasse (n : ℕ) : SimpleGraph (Finset (Fin n)) :=\n  hasse _\n\n\nThe Hamming cube construction is also easy to give and easier to work with than the\nHasse diagram.\n\nThe Hamming cube definition of the n-dimensional hypercube./-- The Hamming cube definition of the n-dimensional hypercube. -/\ndef hypercubeHamming (n : ℕ) : SimpleGraph (Fin n → Bool) where\n  Adj x y := hammingDist x y = 1\n  symm := ⟨by simp [hammingDist_comm]⟩\n\n\nBut for our purposes the most useful construction of the hypercube is as the box\nproduct of n copies of K₂.\n\nThe box product construction of the hypercube requires a bit more setup, but will be\nworth it. The reason we choose this construction is that it is recursive in n:\nits vertex type is built so that Q[n + 1] = Q[n] □ K₂ holds definitionally. Almost\nevery result we prove about the hypercube in the later chapters is by induction on\nn, and this recursive structure is exactly what allows these to work. It\nalso lets us reuse Mathlib's box product lemmas directly.\n\nFor the box product construction we need to first define the vertex type of the\nhypercube. There are two things we need to decide: firstly what should the vertex\ntype of Q[0] be? Since Q[0] is a single vertex we will use the canonical type\nwith exactly one element, Unit.\n\n#check Unit\n\n\nSecondly, when we said Q[n + 1] = Q[n] □ K₂ we need to choose a vertex type for\nK₂. We will choose the type Bool with its two elements, false and true.\n\n#source Bool\n\n\nThe vertex type of the n-dimensional hypercube (box product construction)./-- The vertex type of the n-dimensional hypercube (box product construction). -/\n@[implicit_reducible]\ndef HypercubeVert : ℕ → Type\n  | 0 => Unit                       -- The canonical type with exactly one element\n  | n + 1 => HypercubeVert n × Bool -- The previous vertex type times Bool\n\n\n(Note that HypercubeVert n is really just Fin n → Bool but defined so that the\nrecursion holds definitionally. We can still easily take the Hamming distance\nviewpoint and we will equip it with the Hamming distance when needed in\nHamming Paths.)\n\nThe @[implicit_reducible] attribute lets Lean unfold HypercubeVert (n + 1) to\nHypercubeVert n × Bool when it checks that implicit arguments have the expected\ntype (as simp does before rewriting), while keeping HypercubeVert opaque to\ninstance search so that the instances we provide for HypercubeVert n in\nComputations with Hypercubes are still found.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma hypercubeVert_zero : HypercubeVert 0 = Unit := rfl\n\n\nlemma hypercubeVert_succ (n : ℕ) :\n    HypercubeVert (n + 1) = (HypercubeVert n × Bool) := rfl\n\n\nThe n-dimensional hypercube can now be defined recursively as the box product of\nthe previous hypercube with K₂, the complete graph on two vertices (where Bool is\nthe vertex type of K₂).\n\nThe n-dimensional hypercube defined as the box product of n copies of K₂./-- The n-dimensional hypercube defined as the box product of n copies of K₂.\n-/\ndef hypercube : (n : ℕ) → SimpleGraph (HypercubeVert n)\n    -- The empty graph on one vertex\n  | 0 => ⊥\n    -- The box product of the n-hypercube with K₂ on Bool\n  | n + 1 => (hypercube n).boxProd ⊤\n\n\nWe will use Q[n] as notation for the n-dimensional hypercube.\n\nThe 4-dimensional hypercube Q_4\n\n@[inherit_doc hypercube]\nscoped notation \"Q[\" n \"]\" => hypercube n\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma hypercube_zero : Q[0] = ⊥ := rfl\n\n\nlemma hypercube_succ (n : ℕ) : Q[n + 1] = Q[n].boxProd ⊤ := rfl\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma hypercube_succ_adj {n : ℕ} {u v : HypercubeVert (n + 1)} :\n    Q[n + 1].Adj u v ↔ (Q[n].Adj u.1 v.1 ∧ u.2 = v.2) ∨ (u.2 ≠ v.2 ∧ u.1 = v.1) :=\n  Iff.rfl\n\n\nBoth HypercubeVert and hypercube are defined by recursion on n, so above we\nrecorded their defining equations as the lemmas hypercubeVert_zero,\nhypercubeVert_succ, hypercube_zero and hypercube_succ, together with\nhypercube_succ_adj, which spells out adjacency in Q[n + 1]. All five hold by\nrfl, but only the two _zero lemmas and hypercube_succ_adj are marked as\n@[simp].\n\nThe _zero lemmas replace HypercubeVert 0 and Q[0] by Unit and ⊥, which\nsimp already knows a lot about, so they can only make a goal simpler. For example,\nsimp can prove ¬ Q[0].Adj u v by chaining hypercube_zero with bot_adj.\n\nexample (u v : HypercubeVert 0) : ¬ Q[0].Adj u v := by simp\n\n\nThe _succ lemmas just unfold the definition by one step, and we leave them out of\nthe simp set, as Mathlib does for recursive definitions (e.g. pow_zero is a\nsimp lemma but pow_succ is not). simp simplifies the parts of a term before the\nterm itself, so if hypercube_succ were a simp lemma it would turn Q[n + 1] into\nQ[n] □ ⊤ before any lemma about the hypercube got a chance to apply.\n\nThe exception is hypercube_succ_adj. It also unfolds a step, but only inside the\nproposition Q[n + 1].Adj u v, which simp can rewrite wherever it occurs, and it\nleaves the graph Q[n + 1] and its vertex type alone. Its right-hand side is what\nMathlib's simp lemmas boxProd_adj and top_adj would give if simp could see\nthat Q[n + 1] is a box product. With it, simp can check adjacency between\nexplicit vertices of Q[n] one coordinate at a time.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Constructions","header":"3.4. Hypercube","id":"/Constructions/#section-hypercube"},"/Walks/#section-nil":{"contents":"As we noted at the start of this chapter, the type of a walk encodes its endpoints.\nWe have already seen examples of how this allows for \"correct by construction\"\ndefinitions (such as Walk.append), but it also has a cost. Simply stating the\nproposition that two walks p : G.Walk u v and q : G.Walk x y are equal is only\npossible if they have precisely the same endpoints. This kind of problem arises\nfrequently when working with dependent types such as G.Walk u v, which depend on\nvalues (here the endpoints).\n\nWe first deal with the special case when one of the walks is nil, before turning to\nthe more general problem of endpoint mismatches with Walk.copy.\n\nThere are two natural ways to state a result about non-nil walks, i.e. walks that\nhave at least one edge. The first is to make the cons constructor explicit in the\nstatement of the result.\n\nexample (h : G.Adj u v) (p : G.Walk v w) : 0 < (p.cons h).length := by\n  simp\n\n\nBut this is not always convenient: it forces any caller of this result to have an\nexplicit cons h p walk, whereas often we have some opaque walk q which we know is\nnot nil, but whose first edge we don't know.\n\nThe second is to hypothesize that the walk is not nil. This is potentially\nproblematic: if the result is about a walk p : G.Walk v w then p ≠ nil does not\ntype-check since nil has type G.Walk u u for some u.\n\n/- error: Type mismatch\n  nil\nhas type\n  Walk ?m.3 ?m.4 ?m.4\nbut is expected to have type\n  G.Walk v w\n-/\nexample (p : G.Walk v w) (h : p ≠ nil) : 0 < p.length := by sorry\n\n\nThe solution is to use the predicate ¬ p.Nil instead.\n\n#source Walk.Nil -- inductive Nil : {v w : V} → G.Walk v w → Prop\n                 --   | nil {u : V} : Nil (nil : G.Walk u u)\n\n\nTo understand how this all works, read on. Otherwise feel free to skip to the next\nsubsection Copy.\n\nRecall that Walk is an inductive type with two constructors: nil (whose type\nforces its two endpoints to coincide) and cons (which does not). Now suppose we\nhave a walk p : G.Walk v w and we try the naive test p = nil. For this equation\nto type-check, Lean must unify the type of nil, namely G.Walk u u, with the type\nof p, namely G.Walk v w, which forces v and w to be defeq. So p = nil\ncannot even be stated unless v and w are already defeq.\n\nNil sidesteps this by being a Prop-valued predicate indexed by the walk. The\nvertices {v w : V} appearing in its definition are indices, free to vary from one\nconstructor to the next, and its single constructor\n| nil {u : V} : Nil (nil : G.Walk u u) fixes both of them to be the same vertex\nu.\n\nTwo things follow immediately from this. Firstly, Nil p is a well-typed proposition\nfor every p : G.Walk v w, whatever its endpoints: so we have replaced an equation\nthat may not type-check with a predicate that always does. Secondly, the only way to\nbuild a proof of Nil p is via this constructor, which applies precisely when p is\nnil. There is no constructor mentioning cons, so Nil (cons h q) is simply\nuninhabited.\n\nThe payoff comes when we eliminate a hypothesis h : p.Nil. If we use cases h\nthere is only one case! In this single case Lean must unify the indices v, w with\nthe constructor's u, so here v and w are defeq. Hence destructing Nil hands\nus back exactly the propositional equality v = w that the dependent type was\nhiding.\n\nexample (p : G.Walk v w) (h : p.Nil) : v = w := by\n  cases h with\n  | nil => -- here the goal is v = v\n    rfl\n\n\nNotice that the proof of Nil.eq is exactly this same pattern matching.\n\n#source Walk.Nil.eq -- lemma Nil.eq {p : G.Walk v w} : p.Nil → v = w | .nil => rfl\n\n\nMoreover, the fact that a cons walk is not Nil can be proved by nofun. There\nare simply no cases to consider!\n\nexample {h : G.Adj u v} {p : G.Walk v w} : ¬ (cons h p).Nil := by nofun\n\n\nNil is essentially a bespoke Eq for walks: both are single-constructor inductive\ntypes indexed by values, whose single constructor forces the indices to coincide.\n\n#source Eq  -- inductive Eq : α → α → Prop where\n            --  | refl (a : α) : Eq a a\n\n\nFinally, we can prove that a non-nil walk has positive length as follows.\n\nexample (p : G.Walk v w) (hn : ¬ p.Nil) : 0 < p.length := by\n  cases p with\n  | nil => exact hn.elim Nil.nil\n  | cons _ _ => simp\n\n\nThe Mathlib API also relates Nil to length directly.\n\n#check Walk.length_eq_zero_iff -- p.length = 0 ↔ p.Nil\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Walks\u0009Plumbing: Fixing Type Mismatches","header":"8.5.1. Nil","id":"/Walks/#section-nil"}});